1895 0 obj
<</Title(This study set out to map how downforce, drag and the flow change with ride height for a single front-wing element, find where downforce peaks and why it falls after, and turn that into a ride-height recommendation, using a mesh and solver set-up validated against reference data first)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1849 0 R/Prev 1894 0 R/Next 1874 0 R>>
endobj

1896 0 obj
<</Title(SST treats the whole boundary layer as turbulent, so skin friction is overstated compared to a real wing that stays laminar near the nose. Against the fully turbulent reference, it's +12%. The trends are what the study relies on.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1874 0 R>>
endobj

1897 0 obj
<</Title(Velocity vectors, h/c 0.06: the slow, reversed region behind mid-chord feeds a wide wake.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1824 0 R/Prev 1876 0 R/Next 1877 0 R>>
endobj

1898 0 obj
<</Title(Closing the gap speeds up the air under the wing, which adds suction. But the air then has to slow back down before the trailing edge, and the tighter the gap, the harder that recovery. Below about 44 mm the separated zone grows faster than the gap suction, so downforce falls and drag climbs)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 1877 0 R/Next 1899 0 R>>
endobj

